1. Code from other projects
---------------------------

src/model/wavelet.py
    create_wavelet_filter, wavelet_transform and inverse_wavelet_transform
    are adapted from WTConv (modified).
    https://github.com/BGU-CS-VIL/WTConv
    Paper: Finder et al., "Wavelet Convolutions for Large Receptive
    Fields", ECCV 2024.
    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2024 Shahaf Finder, Roy Amoyal,
    Eran Treister, Oren Freifeld. Full text in section 4.

src/model/fcc_modules.py
    The FCC-Net decoder (the octave convolutions, Octave, SCF, CCM, their
    helper layers and the wiring packaged as FCCDecoder) is ported from the
    FCC-Net reference code (modified). RegionPrototypeAttention,
    FullResHead and BoundaryRegionCalib are ours.
    https://github.com/taozh2017/FCC-Net
    Paper: Tang et al., "Frequency-Enhanced Contextual Conversion
    Network for Esophageal Lesion Segmentation", Pattern Recognition,
    vol. 171, 112235, 2026.
    That repository publishes no licence.
    FCC-Net's FirstOctaveConv, OctaveConv and LastOctaveConv are based on
    OctaveConv_pytorch.
    https://github.com/lxtGH/OctaveConv_pytorch
    Paper: Chen et al., "Drop an Octave: Reducing Spatial Redundancy in
    Convolutional Neural Networks with Octave Convolution", ICCV 2019.
    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2019 XingtaiLi. Full text in section 4.

src/model/contrast.py
    ProjectionHead and PixelContrastLoss are ported from CTNet (modified).
    https://github.com/Fhujinwu/CTNet
    Paper: Xiao et al., "CTNet: Contrastive Transformer Network for Polyp
    Segmentation", IEEE Transactions on Cybernetics, vol. 54, no. 9,
    pp. 5040-5053, 2024.
    That repository publishes no licence.
    CTNet's PixelContrastLoss is based on the one in ContrastiveSeg.
    https://github.com/tfzhou/ContrastiveSeg
    Paper: Wang et al., "Exploring Cross-Image Pixel Contrast for Semantic
    Segmentation", ICCV 2021.
    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2020 openseg-group (Yuhui Yuan,JingyiXie,
    Jianyuan Guo,Lang Huang). Full text in section 4.

src/train.py
    The boundary-weighted BCE + IoU loss in _seg_loss_pranet follows
    structure_loss from F3Net (modified).
    https://github.com/weijun88/F3Net
    Paper: Wei et al., "F3Net: Fusion, Feedback and Focus for Salient
    Object Detection", AAAI 2020.
    MIT License. Copyright (c) 2019 Jun Wei. Full text in section 4.

src/model/mamba_ref.py
    Mamba follows the Mamba module of mamba-ssm (same parameters and
    forward path), and selective_scan follows its selective_scan_ref,
    rewritten as a pure-PyTorch chunked scan in place of the CUDA kernel
    (modified).
    https://github.com/state-spaces/mamba
    Paper: Gu and Dao, "Mamba: Linear-Time Sequence Modeling with
    Selective State Spaces", arXiv:2312.00752, 2023.
    Apache License 2.0. Copyright (c) 2023, Tri Dao, Albert Gu.
    Full text in section 5.

src/model/polyp_pvt.py
    A port of Polyp-PVT (modified), kept as the comparison baseline.
    src/train.py builds it only under --model polyp_pvt; the SpectraMambaNet
    model and the released weights do not use it.
    https://github.com/DengPingFan/Polyp-PVT
    Paper: Dong et al., "Polyp-PVT: Polyp Segmentation with Pyramid
    Vision Transformers", CAAI Artificial Intelligence Research, vol. 2,
    9150015, 2023.
    That repository has no licence file. Its README states: "The source
    code is free for research and education use only. Any comercial use
    should get formal permission first."


2. Pretrained weights
---------------------

Training starts the encoder from PVT's ImageNet PVTv2-B2 weights, loaded
through timm. The released checkpoint contains those weights, modified by
fine-tuning.
    PVT: https://github.com/whai362/PVT
    Paper: Wang et al., "PVT v2: Improved Baselines with Pyramid Vision
    Transformer", Computational Visual Media, vol. 8, no. 3,
    pp. 415-424, 2022.
    Apache License 2.0. Its LICENSE file carries the notice
    "Copyright 2020 - present, Facebook, Inc". Full text in section 5.
    timm: https://github.com/huggingface/pytorch-image-models
    Apache License 2.0.


3. Data and libraries
---------------------

The released weights were trained on Kvasir-SEG and CVC-ClinicDB images.
Kvasir-SEG's terms limit it to research and education and forbid
commercial use without its providers' prior written permission. Check the
terms of both datasets before any commercial use of the weights.
    Kvasir-SEG paper: Jha et al., "Kvasir-SEG: A Segmented Polyp
    Dataset", MultiMedia Modeling (MMM), 2020.
    CVC-ClinicDB paper: Bernal et al., "WM-DOVA Maps for Accurate Polyp
    Highlighting in Colonoscopy: Validation vs. Saliency Maps from
    Physicians", Computerized Medical Imaging and Graphics, vol. 43,
    pp. 99-111, 2015.

Images in assets/ include frames from the public polyp benchmarks. Those
frames stay under their providers' terms and are not covered by the MIT
License.

The libraries in requirements.txt and requirements_colab.txt are installed
separately and used under their own licences. Apart from the mamba-ssm
code in section 1, none of their code is copied into this repository.


